[Event "Imbalanced endgames: the bishop that cannot run"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Role "teaching"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/d2Tnwe56/BfuMfBOr"]
[FEN "8/8/6p1/8/7p/3K4/7R/3kb3 b - - 3 55"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/6p1/8/7p/3K4/7R/3kb3_b_-_-_3_55"]

{Welcome to imbalanced endgames. An imbalance is simple to spot: the two sides hold different kinds of pieces, so counting points will not tell you who is winning — only whether those pieces can do their job.}
55... g5 {Today the imbalance is your rook against his bishop and two pawns. His king and bishop have not moved an inch, so send your rook all the way to a2, the corner square his king can never reach. [%cal Yh2a2]} 56. Ra2 {Your rook lands on a2, further from his king than a rook usually needs to be, and from there it can start cutting off every square his bishop might run to.} (56. Rxh4 {Grabbing the pawn on h4 looks tempting, but his pawn on g5 guards that square, and your rook is about to be lost for nothing.} 56... gxh4 {His g pawn recaptures on h4, and your rook is gone for a single pawn. A bare king cannot hold this position at all. [%cal Rg5h4]}) (56. Rd2+ {The check lands your rook on d2, right beside his bishop on e1 — and a bishop simply takes a rook parked next to it.} 56... Bxd2 {His bishop takes the rook on d2, and with no rook left you cannot stop his king, bishop and pawns at all. [%cal Re1d2]}) (56. Rc2 {The rook creeps to c2, but that square sits one column too close to his king for the box you are building — his bishop gets loose.} 56... Bg3 {The bishop slips out to g3, and once it is loose your rook can no longer trap it or catch both pawns in time. A draw. [%csl Gg3]}) (56. Rb2 {The rook drops to b2, one column too close — his king simply marches up and attacks it there.} 56... Kc1 {His king steps to c1, right next to your rook on b2, and the rook must run. The tempo you needed to trap the bishop is gone. A draw. [%cal Rc1b2]}) (56. Re2 {The rook goes to e2, attacking the bishop head-on — but his king stands right beside it and simply defends.} 56... Bc3 {The bishop slides off the row to c3, safe behind his king's defence, and the win has slipped away. A draw. [%csl Gc3]}) (56. Rh1 {The rook swings to h1 to attack the bishop again, and again his king is standing guard right beside it.} 56... g4 {His pawns start running while your rook is still chasing the bishop, and now you must chase them instead. A draw. [%cal Rg4g3]}) 56... Kc1 {He tucks his king toward the corner on c1, but his bishop still has not moved. Check him again from long range with the rook to a1. [%cal Ya2a1]} 57. Ra1+ {Check from a1 keeps your rook far away, and his king still cannot get anywhere near it to chase it off.} (57. Rc2+ {Another check, this time from c2 — but it lets his king slip out toward the bishop instead of staying boxed in.} 57... Kb1 {His king steps to b1, and next move the bishop runs free with nothing left to stop it. A draw. [%csl Ge1]}) (57. Rb2 {Careful — b2 sits right beside his king, and a king simply takes a rook parked next to it.} 57... Kxb2 {His king takes the rook on b2, and with the rook gone the whole game is gone too. [%cal Rc1b2]}) (57. Ra3 {The rook waits on a3, but he does not have to wait with you — his bishop gets to move instead.} 57... Bg3 {Out goes the bishop to g3, and the win goes with it. A draw. [%csl Gg3]}) (57. Ke2 {The king comes closer to help, but that takes the lid off the box — his bishop can slip out with the king standing guard.} 57... Bg3 {The bishop steps out to g3 for good, and with his king standing right beside it, you can never box it in again. A draw. [%csl Gg3]}) 57... Kb2 {His king is forced to b2, right beside your rook — and the only safe square left is the one his bishop is sitting on. Rook takes on e1. [%cal Ya1e1]} 58. Rxe1 {The bishop falls on e1. That is the whole idea of an imbalance: two lone pawns are no match for a king and rook working together. [%csl Ge1][%tail]} 58... h3 59. Rh1 59... g4 60. Rg1 60... h2 61. Rg2+ 61... Ka3 62. Rxh2 62... Kb4 63. Rg2 63... Kc5 64. Rxg4 {Both pawns are gone, and only bare kings and your rook remain on the board — a king and rook against a bare king always wins.} 1-0


[Event "Imbalanced endgames: the rook in the net"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/d2Tnwe56/2qMX8ghX"]
[FEN "7R/8/5p2/4nkp1/7p/5P2/6K1/8 w - - 6 58"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/7R/8/5p2/4nkp1/7p/5P2/6K1/8_w_-_-_6_58"]

{His rook has chased your pawns deep into your own camp on h6, but he forgot to count — your knight on e5 backs up all three pawns at once.}
58. Rh6 {The rook drops to h6, deep in your half of the board and a long way from his own king. [%csl Rh6]} 58... Nf7 {The knight jumps to f7, attacking the rook on h6 and guarding h8 at the same time — the rook has nowhere safe to run. [%csl Gh6,Gh8]} (58... Nxf3 {Grabbing the pawn on f3 wins material, but it drops the knight right beside his king.} 59. Kxf3 {His king simply takes the knight on f3, and now your three pawns have no piece left to defend them.}) (58... Ng4 {The knight jumps to g4 instead, but there is no pawn there to win — it only walks into the reach of the f3 pawn.} 59. fxg4+ {His f pawn captures the knight on g4, with check, and now three pawns alone have to hold off his rook and pawn. That is lost. [%cal Rf3g4]}) (58... Ng6 {The knight steps to g6, a tidy-looking square, but it does nothing to the rook on h6 at all.} 59. Kf2 {His king strolls out to f2 and the rook slides free — the win is gone, and the position is level again.}) (58... h3+ {The h pawn runs on with check instead, but it never actually catches the rook.} 59. Kg3 {His king simply blocks the pawn on g3, and now the rook is loose again. The position is level. [%csl Rh3]}) (58... Kf4 {The king marches forward to f4, hunting for something to attack, but that abandons the net around the rook.} 59. Rxf6+ {His rook grabs the pawn on f6, with check, and slips out of the net at the same time. Level again.}) 59. Rh7 {The rook slides to h7, the last open square left on the file for it to run to.} 59... Kg6 {Your king steps up to attack the rook on h7, your knight from f7 still keeps it boxed in, and now the rook has nowhere at all to go. [%csl Rh7]} (59... Ne5 {Bringing the knight back to the centre is usually right, but here it opens the cage and lets the rook slip out.} 60. Ra7 {His rook runs free along the seventh row to a7. The position is level again.}) (59... h3+ {The pawn checks again from h3, but that only hands the rook something to capture on its way out.} 60. Rxh3 {His rook captures the pawn on h3 and escapes down the file with it, a pawn richer. The position is level. [%cal Rh7h3]}) (59... Nh6 {Moving the knight to h6 looks like it keeps watch nearby, but that square is not safe — his rook simply takes it there.} 60. Rxh6 {His rook captures the knight on h6 outright, and without it nothing traps him any more. That is lost. [%cal Rh7h6]}) (59... Kf4 {Your king runs the wrong way toward f4 and leaves the knight on f7 undefended.} 60. Rxf7 {His rook captures the knight on f7 for nothing, and one lone pawn was never going to hold a rook back on its own. That is lost. [%cal Rh7f7]}) (59... g4 {Pushing the pawn to g4 looks active, but the knight on f7 was the piece actually holding the net shut.} 60. Rxf7 {His rook takes the knight on f7 anyway, and the net you built falls apart. That is lost.}) 60. Rxh4 {He gives the rook up for a pawn on h4 rather than watch it die for nothing in the net.} 60... gxh4 {Your pawn recaptures on h4. A knight and two pawns against his one lone pawn — that is winning. [%csl Gh4]} (60... Ne5 {Leaving his rook alive instead of taking it lets him tuck it away to safety.} 61. Rh1 {His rook reaches safety on h1, and one pawn on its own is not enough to win. A draw.}) (60... Kg7 {Stepping the king politely aside to g7 lets his rook simply walk away.} 61. Rh1 {His rook escapes to h1 all the same, and the win you had is gone. A draw.}) (60... g4 {Pushing the pawn past the rook instead of taking it hands him a free target.} 61. Rxg4+ {His rook captures the pawn on g4, with check, and now you are the one who is lost. [%cal Rh4g4]}) 0-1


[Event "Imbalanced endgames: lure the rook onto the diagonal"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/d2Tnwe56/tHXGwlH0"]
[FEN "8/8/6P1/8/4k1BK/r3p3/6P1/8 b - - 0 55"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/6P1/8/4k1BK/r3p3/6P1/8_b_-_-_0_55"]

{Just a bishop against his rook today, and your pawn on g6 is nearly home — only one order of moves gets it there safely.}
55... Ra5 {His rook swings across the board to a5, racing to reach the back row before your pawn can queen. [%csl Ra5]} 56. g7 {Push to g7. His rook must reach the eighth row to deal with the coming queen, and a8 is the only square it can reach — right on the diagonal your bishop is about to enter. [%csl Ga8]} (56. Bf3+ {Checking from f3 is the right idea, just one move early — his rook has not reached the diagonal yet, so the check achieves nothing lasting.} 56... Kf4 {He simply steps up to f4. Your pawn is still stuck on g6 and his rook is still free — only a draw. [%csl Rg6]}) (56. g3 {A quiet pawn push to g3 does nothing at all for the runner on g6.} 56... Ra1 {His rook swings behind your pawn to a1, and from there it can stop the pawn moving again for good. A draw.}) (56. Bf5+ {The check on f5 throws the bishop away for nothing.} 56... Kxf5 {His king simply captures the bishop on f5, and now you are the one who is lost. [%cal Re4f5]}) (56. Kg3 {Bringing your king back to g3 to help the pawn looks natural, but it walks into a pin.} 56... Rg5 {His rook pins your bishop to your king along the g file and wins it outright. You are lost. [%cal Rg5g3]}) (56. Bh5 {Guarding the pawn from behind on h5 looks safe, but the bishop and the pawn both fall to the same move.} 56... Rxh5+ {His rook captures the bishop on h5, with check, and the pawn falls next. A draw. [%cal Ra5h5]}) 56... Ra8 {a8 is the only square that stops the pawn from queening unanswered, so his rook has to sit there.} 57. Bf3+ {Check! He has to answer, and from f3 your bishop looks straight down the diagonal to a8. [%cal Gf3a8]} (57. Kg5 {Walking your king up to escort the pawn instead of checking wastes the moment.} 57... Rb8 {His rook waits on the eighth row for ever, and you can never push the pawn past it. A draw.}) (57. Be2 {Stepping the bishop aside to e2 lets the pawn run, but it gives up the diagonal to a8 completely.} 57... Rg8 {His rook parks right in front of the pawn on g8, and now it can never queen. You are lost. [%csl Rg8]}) (57. Bf5+ {The check on f5 gives the bishop away again, and the bishop is what you needed to win the rook.} 57... Kxf5 {His king captures the bishop on f5 for nothing, and his rook still sits safely on a8 watching the square. You are lost. [%cal Re4f5]}) (57. Bh5 {Guarding from h5 on the other side runs into the very same answer as before.} 57... Rg8 {His rook slides to g8 all the same, right in front of the pawn, and your pawn is dead. You are lost. [%csl Rg8]}) 57... Kd4 {He steps his king away from the check, and the diagonal to a8 stays wide open for your bishop.} 58. Bxa8 {The bishop captures the rook on a8. A bishop with two pawns is now an army against his lone king and pawn. [%csl Ga8][%tail]} (58. Kg5 {Escorting the pawn with your king instead of capturing the rook lets him hold the eighth row.} 58... Rb8 {He holds the eighth row on b8, and you can never push the pawn past him there. A draw.}) (58. Bg4 {Tucking the bishop away to g4 declines the free rook on a8 and lets it live.} 58... Rg8 {His rook slides across to g8 and stops the pawn dead in its tracks. You are lost. [%csl Rg8]}) (58. Be4 {Offering a trade on e4 gives away the very bishop you need to win the rook.} 58... Kxe4 {His king captures the bishop on e4 for free, and now you are the one who is lost. [%cal Rd4e4]}) 58... e2 59. g8=Q 59... e1=Q+ 60. Kh3 {Your king tucks safely into the corner on h3, and a queen with a bishop in support beats a lone queen every time. That is winning.} 1-0


[Event "Imbalanced endgames: chase the bishop, win the knight"]
[Result "1-0"]
