[FEN "3k4/8/2pb4/7R/1p2K3/8/1PP1nP2/8 b - - 0 43"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/3k4/8/2pb4/7R/1p2K3/8/1PP1nP2/8_b_-_-_0_43"]

{A new theme: two minor pieces take on a lone rook. Side by side a bishop and knight are strong, but easy prey the moment they drift apart.}
43... Nf4 {His knight jumps to f4 and looks annoying, but his bishop and knight are now a long way apart from each other.} 44. Rh6 {The rook lands on the sixth row on h6, and the bishop on d6 has to run before the rook eats it next. [%cal Gh6d6]} (44. Rh4 {Chasing the knight with the rook to h4 lets it simply step away untouched.} 44... Nd5 45. Kd4 Ke7 {The knight settles safely on d5 and his king reaches e7 — his pieces find a home, and the game is level.}) (44. Rh8+ {The check on h8 costs a tempo for nothing — his king is happy to walk out of it.} 44... Kc7 45. Rh7+ Kb6 {The checks run their course and his king reaches b6 with nothing changed. Still level.}) (44. Re5 {Offering a trade on e5 in the middle of the board lets him swap pieces on his own terms.} 44... Bxe5 45. Kxe5 Nd5 {He captures on e5 and his knight settles on d5 afterward. He swaps with pleasure, and it stays level.}) (44. Rf5 {Attacking the knight from f5 does nothing — it simply steps away to safety.} 44... Ne6 45. Rf6 Ke7 {The knight steps to e6 and his king reaches e7. Both his pieces are safe, and the game stays level.}) 44... Bf8 {The bishop runs for the corner on f8, and his two pieces are still nowhere near each other.} 45. Rf6 {The rook on f6 attacks the bishop on f8 and the knight on f4 down the same file — he can only save one of them. [%cal Gf6f8,Gf6f4]} (45. Rxc6 {Grabbing the pawn on c6 is free material, but it lets both his pieces breathe instead of losing one.} 45... Kd7 46. Rf6 Ne6 {His king steps to d7 and the knight settles safely on e6 — it is safe now, and the win you had has gone. Level.}) (45. Kxf4 {Your king captures the knight on f4, but that leaves your rook on h6 completely undefended.} 45... Bxh6+ {His bishop captures the rook on h6, with check — your rook is gone for nothing, and now you are the one who is lost. [%cal Rf8h6]}) (45. Ke5 {The king marches into the centre on e5, walking straight past a rook that nobody is defending.} 45... Bxh6 {His bishop simply captures the hanging rook on h6. You are lost.}) (45. Rh8 {Retreating the rook to safety on h8 costs a whole move he does not need to give away.} 45... Ne6 46. Kf5 Kd7 {His knight settles on e6 and his king reaches d7 — both his pieces are fine again. Still level.}) 45... Bg7 {He saves the bishop on g7, and on the way it takes aim at your rook.} 46. Rxf4 {The knight falls on f4. A rook and three pawns against a bishop and two pawns is winning for you. [%csl Gf4]} (46. Rxc6 {Taking the other pawn on c6 grabs material, but the knight on f4 was the bigger prize sitting there.} 46... Nh3 47. f4 Bxb2 {His knight slips out to h3 and his bishop mops up the b2 pawn — he wriggles free of everything. Level.}) (46. Rf8+ {The check on f8 hands your own rook straight to his bishop.} 46... Bxf8 {His bishop on g7 captures the rook on f8, and it is simply gone, traded for nothing you needed. You are lost. [%cal Rg7f8]}) (46. Kf5 {Defending the rook with the king on f5 still costs you the exchange.} 46... Bxf6 47. Kxf6 Kd7 {His bishop trades itself for the rook on f6 and his knight survives. You traded a rook for a bishop, and you are lost.}) 1-0


[Event "Imbalanced endgames: the bishop drives, the knight forks"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
