[FEN "8/4bk2/4n3/7K/6P1/8/6R1/8 w - - 21 81"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/4bk2/4n3/7K/6P1/8/6R1/8_w_-_-_21_81"]

{This time the two minor pieces are yours — bishop and knight together, hunting down his lone rook.}
81. Kh6 {His king steps up to h6, right out in the open with your bishop and knight both nearby. [%csl Rh6]} 81... Bf8+ {Check! The bishop on f8 shoves his king down a row, and that row has a knight fork waiting on it. [%cal Gf8h6]} (81... Nf4 {Attacking the rook straight away with the knight lets it simply step aside with no check behind it.} 82. Rg1 {His rook steps aside to g1, safe, and with no check to answer you have gained nothing at all. A draw.}) (81... Bg5+ {The check from g5 is the wrong one — his king can simply attack the bishop from where it lands.} 82. Kh5 {His king steps to h5 and now attacks your bishop on g5, so the fork you wanted would just hang it. A draw. [%csl Rg5]}) (81... Bd6 {A quiet retreat to d6 gives up the check and buys his pawn time to run.} 82. g5 {His pawn on g4 gets moving to g5, and the fork you needed never arrives. A draw.}) (81... Nd4 {Sending the knight the long way round through d4 costs too many moves.} 82. g5 {His pawn runs to g5 while your knight is still travelling toward the fork. A draw.}) 82. Kh5 {Two squares were on offer for his king, and he chose the tougher one on h5 to defend.} 82... Nf4+ {Check from f4! The knight lands on the very square that forks his king and the rook together. [%cal Gf4h5,Gf4g2]} (82... Ng7+ {Checking with the other knight from g7 forgets all about the rook entirely.} 83. Kh4 {His king simply walks on to h4, and your knight is standing on the wrong square to do anything about it. A draw.}) (82... Kf6 {Bringing your king up to f6 instead of forking costs you the moment.} 83. g5+ {His pawn checks you instead from g5, and the fork you were building toward is gone. A draw.}) (82... Bd6 {The bishop steps back to d6 to regroup, but that hands his pawn the time it needs to run.} 83. Rg1 {His rook tucks safely into g1, and there is no fork left to find. A draw.}) (82... Nd4 {Heading for g2 the slow way through d4 gives him too much time.} 83. g5 {His pawn simply runs while your knight is still travelling. Too slow, and only a draw.}) 83. Kh4 {Check forces his king out to h4 — there is nowhere else for it to go.} 83... Nxg2+ {The rook on g2 falls to your knight, with check. A bishop and a knight against his one lone pawn on g4 is winning, even if it takes a while. [%csl Gg2]} (83... Ng6+ {Checking with the other knight from g6 forgets the rook on g2 completely.} 84. Kg3 {His king simply steps to g3, and his rook on g2 stays safe. The win is gone — only a draw.}) (83... Be7+ {Checking with the bishop from e7 leaves his rook standing untouched on g2.} 84. Kg3 {His king steps out to g3 and keeps his rook on g2 all the same. A draw.}) (83... Kf6 {Walking your king up to f6 instead of capturing lets his rook stay right where it is.} 84. g5+ {His pawn checks you from g5, and his rook on g2 lives to fight on. A draw.}) 0-1


[Event "Imbalanced endgames: two knights and a pawn"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
