[FEN "8/8/8/5kp1/RP3n2/6K1/3n1P1P/8 w - - 1 41"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/8/5kp1/RP3n2/6K1/3n1P1P/8_w_-_-_1_41"]

{Your two knights and a pawn have to deal with his rook, which has wandered off to escort his own passed pawn on the queenside.}
41. b5 {His pawn runs to b5, and his rook on a4 is now a long way from his own king on g3. [%csl Rg3]} 41... Nf1+ {Check! His king on g3 has exactly one square to go to, and it is the very square you want it on. [%cal Gf1g3]} (41... Ne2+ {Checking with the other knight from e2 gives up the mating idea for nothing extra.} 42. Kg2 Ne4 43. b6 {His king steps to g2, your knight settles on e4, and then his pawn runs to b6 — his pawn is moving and yours is not. You are lost.}) (41... Nh5+ {A check from the other side, on h5, tells the same story as the other knight check.} 42. Kg2 Ne4 43. b6 {The same story plays out: his king steps aside, your knight settles on e4, and the b pawn queens while yours stands still. You are lost.}) (41... Ne4+ {A fork on e4 looks tempting, and he is delighted to simply take it with the rook.} 42. Rxe4 Kxe4 43. b6 {His rook captures the knight on e4, your king recaptures, but his other pawn runs to b6 and queens anyway. You are lost. [%cal Ra4e4]}) (41... Nd5 {Trying to block the pawn with the knight on d5 is too slow to matter.} 42. Rd4 Ne4+ 43. Rxe4 {His rook pins the knight to d4, and after the check it simply captures on e4 — the rook is quicker than two knights. You are in real trouble.}) (41... Nd3 {Sitting the knight down on d3 in front of the pawn instead of giving check wastes the whole idea.} 42. b6 Nc5 43. Ra5 {His pawn runs to b6, your knight chases it to c5, but his rook simply defends from a5. Nothing stops that pawn. You are lost.}) 42. Kf3 {His king has to go to f3 — it is the only square the check on f1 left him.} 42... g4# {Mate! Your pawn delivers it on g4, with both knights and your king covering every square his king might run to. [%csl Ge3,Gg3,Ge2,Gg2]} (42... Nxh2+ {Grabbing the free pawn on h2 with check throws away the mate that was sitting right there.} 43. Ke3 Ng4+ 44. Kf3 {His king slips out to e3, your knight checks again from g4, and his king simply returns to f3 — you have a pawn, and he still has a whole rook. Level.}) (42... Nd2+ {Another check, this time from d2, goes nowhere at all.} 43. Ke3 Nb3 44. Ra3 {His king steps to e3, your knight lands on b3, and his rook simply takes over the board from a3. You are worse.}) (42... Ng3 {Blocking the mating square yourself with the knight on g3 ruins your own mate.} 43. Rxf4+ gxf4 44. fxg3 {His rook captures your knight on f4 with check, your pawn recaptures, and his own pawn mops up on g3. Everything falls apart. You are lost.}) (42... Ke5 {Wandering the king off to e5 instead of finishing the job gives him all the time he needs.} 43. b6 Nxh2+ 44. Kg3 {His pawn runs to b6, your knight grabs a pawn on h2 with check, but his king simply steps to g3 and the pawn queens. You are lost.}) 0-1


[Event "Imbalanced endgames: make his rook stand on the fork square"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
