[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/5k1p/2P5/1B6/1p3Pp1/1N3p2/7r/5K2_b_-_-_0_43"]

{Bishop, knight and a passed pawn are on your side today, and his lone rook cannot watch all of it at once.}
43... Ke6 {His king hurries across to help defend, but every move here except one throws your win away.} 44. c7 {Push the pawn to c7. Now his rook must drop back to c2 to stop it, and c2 is not a safe square for it. [%cal Gc7c8]} (44. Nd4+ {The fork on d4 comes one move too early — his rook has not been forced to c2 yet, so there is nothing there to hit.} 44... Kd6 45. Ba4 Rd2 {His king steps to d6, your bishop retreats to a4, and his rook simply swings round to d2. The knight has to move again, and you are lost.}) (44. Nc5+ {A check from c5 does nothing at all about his rook.} 44... Kd6 45. Ne4+ Kc7 {His king steps to d6, your knight checks again from e4, and his king simply marches on to reach your pawn. You are lost.}) (44. f5+ {A check with the f pawn gives a free tempo away for nothing.} 44... Kd6 45. Nc5 Rc2 {His king steps to d6, your knight lands on c5, but his rook still reaches c2 in time. The pawn falls anyway, and you are lost.}) (44. Bc4+ {Another check, this time with the bishop, has nothing behind it either.} 44... Kd6 45. Nd4 Rd2 {His king steps to d6, your knight lands on d4, but his rook still gets to d2 first. One move too slow, and you are lost.}) (44. Be2 {Blocking his runner pawn on f3 with the bishop looks tidy, but the pawn simply eats it.} 44... fxe2+ {His pawn captures the bishop on e2, with check, and you are lost. [%cal Rf3e2]}) 44... Rc2 {He has no choice but to stop the pawn, and c2 is the only square that does the job.} 45. Nd4+ {The fork! Check on his king and a hit on the rook, both from the same square on d4. [%cal Gd4e6,Gd4c2]} (45. c8=Q {A new queen on c8 looks tempting, but his rook on c2 has been watching that very square up the column all along. [%cal Rc2c8]} 45... Rxc8 {His rook simply captures the new queen on c8, and you are lost. [%cal Rc2c8]}) (45. Bd3 {Attacking the rook with the bishop from d3 instead of forking gives him a free target.} 45... Rxc7 {His rook captures the pawn on c7 for nothing given up in return. You are lost.}) (45. Bc6 {Blocking the file with the bishop on c6 hangs it to the rook.} 45... Rxc6 {His rook simply captures the bishop on c6, and your pawn falls next. You are lost. [%cal Rc2c6]}) (45. Nd2 {Defending the f3 pawn from behind with the knight ignores the rook completely.} 45... Rxc7 {His rook captures the pawn on c7, and the whole point of the plan is gone with it. You are lost.}) 45... Kd6 {He pulls his king back to d6 to save it from the fork.} 46. Nxc2 {The rook falls to your knight on c2. Even if he scoops a pawn or two back, a bishop and a knight together are still far too much for him. [%csl Gc2]} (46. Nf5+ {A second check from f5 instead of capturing the rook lets it live.} 46... Kxc7 {His king simply eats the pawn on c7 and keeps his rook. You are lost.}) (46. Bd3 {Hitting the rook one more time with the bishop from d3 wastes the capture that was already sitting there.} 46... Rc1+ 47. Kf2 Kxc7 {His rook checks first from c1, your king steps to f2, and only then does his king eat the pawn on c7. You are much worse.}) (46. Be8 {Tucking the bishop away to e8 instead of taking the rook lets it slip out with a check.} 46... Rc1+ 47. Kf2 Kxc7 {His rook checks from c1 all the same, your king steps to f2, and his king escapes to eat the pawn on c7. You are lost.}) 1-0


[Event "Imbalanced endgames: let him have the queen"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
