[IntendedResult "win"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/d2Tnwe56/WSQXmDIV"]
[FEN "2R5/8/6P1/8/8/3n1k2/5b2/2r2K2 w - - 0 69"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2R5/8/6P1/8/8/3n1k2/5b2/2r2K2_w_-_-_0_69"]

{A new theme: giving material away on purpose. What you get back for it does not have to be material at all — sometimes it is mate.}
69. Rxc1 {He captures your rook on c1 with his own, and now his pawn on g6 is only two steps away from queening. [%csl Rg6]} 69... Nxc1 {Of course, your knight recaptures on c1. Now his pawn runs free, and there is nothing left that can stop it reaching the eighth row.} 70. g7 {His pawn cannot be stopped, and it queens next move regardless of what you do. Is there something faster than stopping it?} 70... Nb3 {The quiet move! The knight heads for b3, a square his coming queen will not be able to cover in time.} (70... Ne2 {The other quiet square, e2, is close — so close it almost works.} 71. g8=Q {His pawn queens on g8 all the same, and there is no mate here. Only a draw.}) (70... Bd4 {Attacking the pawn at last with the bishop on d4 comes one move too late.} 71. g8=Q {His pawn simply queens on g8 anyway. Too late, and you are lost.}) (70... Ke3 {Marching your king in to e3 to help looks natural, but the queen is coming regardless of it.} 71. g8=Q {His pawn queens on g8 while your net is still not built. You are lost.}) (70... Nd3 {Another knight square, on d3, still does not do the job in time.} 71. g8=Q {His queen arrives on g8 all the same, and that knight move bought you nothing. You are lost.}) 71. g8=Q {He gets his queen on g8, exactly as planned — now the mate has to land before she can do anything about it.} 71... Nd2# {Mate! There is a brand new queen on the board and she cannot save him — your knight and bishop did that together. [%csl Gd2]} (71... Nd4 {A good-looking square, but d4 is not the square that delivers mate.} 72. Qf8+ {His queen starts checking from f8, and now you are the one who is lost.}) (71... Be3 {Setting the bishop up slowly on e3 gives his fresh queen time to act first.} 72. Qxb3 {His queen simply captures the knight on b3, and you are lost. [%cal Rg8b3]}) (71... Ke4 {Stepping the king away to e4 instead of mating leaves your knight hanging.} 72. Qxb3 {The new queen just eats your knight on b3, and you are lost. [%cal Rg8b3]}) 0-1


[Event "Imbalanced endgames: let the knight go"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/d2Tnwe56/fCRh3jrV"]
[FEN "8/8/2P1r3/5p2/1P5N/5k2/3K4/8 b - - 3 50"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/2P1r3/5p2/1P5N/5k2/3K4/8_b_-_-_3_50"]

{Rook and pawn face knight and two pawns today, and right now it is your knight standing in the danger zone.}
50... Kg4 {His king comes hunting for the knight, and the knight has nowhere good left to run to.} 51. b5 {Let the knight go! Two pawns standing side by side on b5 and c6 are worth more than it. [%csl Gb5,Gc6]} (51. Nf3 {Saving the knight by retreating it to f3 looks like the natural move to make.} 51... Kxf3 {His king simply takes the knight on f3 anyway, and now it is only a draw.}) (51. Nxf5 {Grabbing the pawn on f5 with the knight on the way out costs you the knight for nothing.} 51... Kxf5 {His king takes the knight on f5, and it is gone for nothing at all. You are lost. [%cal Rg4f5]}) (51. c7 {Pushing the front pawn to c7 first, before dealing with the knight, lets his rook get ahead of it.} 51... Rc6 {His rook drops in front on c6, and your other pawn on b4 is still miles from home. You are lost.}) (51. Kc3 {Bringing your king across to c3 abandons the c6 pawn completely.} 51... Rxc6+ {His rook captures the pawn on c6, with check, because nothing was left standing behind it. You are lost. [%cal Re6c6]}) (51. Ng6 {Tucking the knight away to safety on g6 saves the piece but not the plan.} 51... f4 {His pawn gets rolling to f4. The knight lives, but the win has gone. Drawn.}) 51... Kxh4 {He takes the knight on h4, of course. Now the order you push your two pawns in matters.} 52. c7 {The front pawn goes first, to c7, and his rook has to run all the way back to stop it. [%cal Gc7c8]} (52. b6 {Pushing the back pawn to b6 first lets his rook simply eat the front one instead.} 52... Rxc6 {His rook captures the front pawn on c6, and it just falls. You are lost. [%cal Re6c6]}) (52. Kc3 {Walking your king up to c3 to help gives him a free move for his own pawn.} 52... f4 {His own pawn gets moving to f4. Only a draw.}) (52. Kd3 {The same idea, one square over on d3, actually loses the game.} 52... f4 {His own pawn rolls to f4 all the same, and this time your king stands one square too far away to matter. You are lost.}) 52... Re8 {He swings his rook to e8, where it covers the queening square along the last row.} 53. b6 {Now you have pawns on the sixth and seventh rows at once, and his rook cannot watch both queening squares from one spot. [%csl Gb6,Gc7][%tail]} (53. c8=Q {A queen at last on c8 — but his rook on e8 was waiting for exactly that all along.} 53... Rxc8 {His rook simply captures the new queen on c8. You are lost. [%cal Re8c8]}) (53. Kc3 {Waiting with the king on c3 hands him the tempo he needs for his own pawn.} 53... f4 {He runs his own pawn to f4 and holds the draw.}) (53. Kd3 {One square wrong again, this time on d3, and it costs the whole game.} 53... f4 {His own pawn runs to f4, and that one square out costs you everything. You are lost.}) 53... Rc8 54. b7 54... Rxc7 55. b8=Q {There is your new queen on b8. Two pawns standing side by side beat a rook that can only ever be in one place.} 1-0


[Event "Imbalanced endgames: clear his king off the promotion square"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Role "exercise"]
