[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/4k3/8/3PP3/1pK1B3/6p1/8/p4P1P/1r6_b_-_-_0_52"]

{His a pawn is one square from a queen, and you have no time at all to worry about it — race him instead, bishop and pawns against his rook and pawns.}
52... Re1 {His rook swings over to e1, but his king still has to watch the d8 square where your pawn wants to queen. [%csl Rd8]} 53. d7+ {Check! Your pawn pushes to d7, and his king can no longer sit safely on e8. [%cal Gd7d8]} (53. Bc3 {Stopping his a pawn with the bishop on c3 looks safe, but it gives up the race entirely.} 53... a1=Q 54. Bxa1 Rxa1 {His pawn queens on a1, your bishop captures it, and his rook simply recaptures on a1. He gives the queen back, but your own pawns are stuck. You are lost.}) (53. Bf6 {Bf6 is the right piece for the job, but his king has not reached e7 yet to be dragged away by it.} 53... Rxe6 {With no check to answer, his rook simply eats the other pawn on e6. You are lost.}) (53. Kd5 {Bringing your king closer on d5 hangs the bishop on e5.} 53... Rxe5+ 54. Kxe5 {His rook captures the bishop on e5, with check, and your king recaptures on e5. You won a rook and lost the game — his a pawn queens regardless.}) (53. f4 {A quiet pawn push to f4 leaves the bishop on e5 hanging.} 53... Rxe5+ {His rook simply grabs the hanging bishop on e5, with check, and the game is gone. [%cal Re1e5]}) 53... Ke7 {He steps his king beside the pawn to e7, and from there it still covers the d8 square. [%cal Re7d8]} 54. Bf6+ {Check from f6 with the bishop! Capturing there is the move that drags his king away from the d8 square guarding your pawn. [%cal Gf6e7]} (54. d8=Q+ {The queen now, on d8 — but look who is standing right next to it.} 54... Kxd8 {His king simply captures the new queen on d8. You are lost. [%cal Re7d8]}) (54. Bd4 {Your bishop drops back to d4, watching the a1 square from a distance instead of checking. [%cal Gd4a1]} 54... Rxe6 {His rook takes the other pawn on e6 while nothing threatens him. You are lost.}) (54. Bc7 {Your bishop lands on c7, covering d8, but that move carries no check at all. [%cal Gc7d8]} 54... Rd1 {His rook swings round to d1 to stop the pawn instead. You are lost.}) (54. Kd5 {The king comes forward again, but his a pawn is simply faster.} 54... a1=Q 55. Bxa1 Rxa1 {His pawn queens on a1, your bishop captures it, but his rook mops up on a1 all the same. The win is gone.}) 54... Kxf6 {His king captures the bishop on f6, and that clears the way for your pawn on d7 to queen with check next.} 55. d8=Q+ {A new queen, with check! He has to move his king, and a queen with three pawns is far too much for a rook and three. [%csl Gd8][%tail]} (55. Kd6 {Escorting the pawn with your king instead of making it queen gives him time to move.} 55... Rd1+ 56. Kc7 a1=Q {His rook checks first from d1, your king steps to c7, and only then does his own pawn queen on a1 — his queen comes first. You are lost.}) (55. Kxb5 {A free pawn on b5, but his own queen is now just one move away.} 55... Ke7 {He stops your d pawn with his king and lets his own pawn run instead. You are lost.}) (55. f4 {A quiet pawn move at the worst possible moment.} 55... Ke7 {His king simply stops your pawn for good. You are lost.}) 55... Kg7 56. Qc7+ 56... Kh6 57. Qf4+ 57... Kh7 58. Qf7+ 58... Kh8 59. Qh5+ 59... Kg8 60. Qxg4+ {The g pawn falls too, with check — a queen and three pawns against a lone rook and two, and his king is still hunting for shelter.} 1-0


[Event "Imbalanced endgames: a rook for the mate"]
[Result "1-0"]
