[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/7r/pnR3p1/k1K2p2/1p6/8/7p/7R_b_-_-_5_46"]

{Both his pawns are one step from queening. Stop counting material altogether — a rook for a knight and then mate is worth more than any pawn.}
46... b3 {His pawn pushes to b3, and both his runners are nearly home. His king now stands right next to yours. [%csl Ra5]} 47. Ra1+ {Check down the a file! Only one piece can get in the way of it. [%cal Ga1a5]} (47. Rxb6 {Taking the knight on b6 looks like the obvious grab.} 47... Rc7+ 48. Rc6 Rxc6+ 49. Kxc6 {His rook checks from c7, your rook blocks on c6, his rook trades itself off there too, and your king recaptures — the checks run out, and his pawns queen. You are lost.}) (47. Rxh2 {Stopping the runner pawn on h2 grabs it, but at the wrong moment.} 47... Rxh2 {His rook simply recaptures on h2, and now he is a whole rook up. You are lost. [%cal Rh7h2]}) (47. Rd1 {Getting behind the h pawn on d1 does not get there in time.} 47... h1=Q {His pawn queens on h1, one move too fast for you to stop it. You are lost.}) (47. Rxg6 {Grabbing the free pawn on g6 while mate was sitting right there wastes the moment.} 47... Rc7+ 48. Kd6 Rc2 {His rook checks from c7, your king steps to d6, and his rook swings round to c2 — his pawns come home. You are lost.}) 47... Na4+ {Forced, with check — his knight has exactly one square to block on.} 48. Rxa4+ {A rook for a knight, taken with check, and his king has no choice but to recapture. [%cal Ga4a5]} (48. Kc4 {Stepping the king to c4 instead of capturing the knight lets his attack keep rolling forward.} 48... Rh4+ 49. Kxb3 h1=Q {His rook checks from h4, your king grabs the pawn on b3, but his own pawn queens on h1 regardless. His queen arrives, and you are lost.}) (48. Kd6 {Running the king away to d6 instead of capturing gives up the tempo completely.} 48... h1=Q {His pawn simply queens on h1. A queen, when mate was only one move away for you. You are lost.}) (48. Kd5 {The same mistake, one square over on d5, still ignores the capture.} 48... h1=Q+ {d5 does nothing about the h pawn either, and it queens with check.}) 48... Kxa4 {He has no choice but to recapture, and his king steps onto a4.} 49. Rxa6# {Mate! You gave a rook for a knight and got the only thing that actually counts. [%csl Ga6]} (49. Rb6 {So close — but the a file was the mating line, not b6.} 49... a5 {His pawn slips through to a5, and he wriggles out. You are lost.}) (49. Kc4 {Forgetting the mate entirely and stepping the king to c4 lets his attack take over.} 49... Rh4+ 50. Kc5 b2 {His rook checks from h4, your king steps to c5, and his pawn runs to b2 — his pawns queen. You are lost.}) (49. Rxg6 {Grabbing a pawn on g6 instead of delivering mate is no substitute for it.} 49... Rc7+ 50. Kd4 h1=Q {His rook checks from c7, driving your king to d4, and his h pawn queens anyway — grabbing a pawn was never worth as much as the mate.}) 1-0


