{A new theme: how much is a pawn really worth against a piece? Your bishop on c3 already has its eye on the corner square h8.}
66. Rh8 {His rook drops into the corner on h8, right on the diagonal your bishop is already watching. [%csl Rh8]} 66... e2 {Your pawn advances to e2, offering itself. If he leaves it alone it simply queens next move, but capturing drags his king one step closer to the corner.} (66... Kf3 {Walking your king in to f3 instead of pushing the pawn lets his rook check you straight back out.} 67. Rf8+ {His rook checks you from f8 and pushes you straight back. Level.}) (66... Bb2 {Regrouping the bishop to b2 gives his own pawn a free tempo.} 67. h3+ {His pawn checks from h3, and your pawns are still stuck where they started. Level.}) (66... Be1 {Another quiet bishop move, to e1, wastes the same tempo.} 67. h3+ {The same check on h3 shrugs off your quiet move all the same. Level.}) (66... Bd2 {Guarding the e pawn with the bishop on d2 gives up the initiative entirely.} 67. Rc8 {His rook takes over the open file from c8. Level.}) 67. Kxe2 {His king captures the pawn on e2, stepping right where your plan needed it to go.} 67... d3+ {There it is — check! The line to the corner is open now, and his rook is sitting right at the end of it. [%cal Gc3h8]} (67... Ba5 {Saving the bishop on a5 protects a piece that was never actually in danger.} 68. Kd3 {His king simply blocks the pawn on d3, and your bishop never gets to see the corner at all. You are lost.}) (67... Kf4 {Bringing your king over to f4 to help does nothing while his rook still sits in the corner.} 68. Rf8+ {His rook checks you from f8, and the moment is gone. You are lost.}) (67... Be1 {Offering the bishop on e1 instead of checking hands it straight to him.} 68. Kxe1 {His king simply captures the bishop on e1. You are lost. [%cal Re2e1]}) (67... Bd2 {The same idea one square along, on d2, meets the same answer.} 68. Kxd2 {His king takes the bishop on d2. You are lost.}) 68. Kxd3 {His king captures the pawn on d3, edging one square closer to the corner your bishop has been eyeing all along.} 68... Bxh8 {Your bishop captures the rook on h8. Two pawns for a rook, with his c pawn going nowhere, is a winning trade. [%csl Gh8]} (68... Be5 {A lovely-looking square, but the rook in the corner was the actual prize.} 69. h3+ {His pawn checks you from h3, and now you are the one who is lost.}) (68... Kf4 {Walking your king away from the corner lets his own pawn get moving.} 69. Kxc3 {His king simply captures the bishop on c3, and it falls. You are lost. [%cal Rd3c3]}) (68... Bb4 {Anywhere but the corner still leaves the rook alive.} 69. c3 {His pawn runs to c3, and you are lost.}) 0-1


[Event "Imbalanced endgames: two pawns walk past a rook"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
