[FEN "8/5p2/4p3/4Pk2/7p/4KPp1/6R1/8 w - - 2 45"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/5p2/4p3/4Pk2/7p/4KPp1/6R1/8_w_-_-_2_45"]

{A piece is only worth what it can actually catch. His rook is about to find out it cannot catch two runners at once.}
45. f4 {His pawn pushes to f4, shutting your king out of the centre of the board.} 45... Kg4 {Your king stands guard on g4 so neither pawn can be captured. Now they can start walking. [%csl Gg4]} (45... Kg6 {Retreating the king to g6 instead of guarding the pawns gives his king the time it needs.} 46. Kf3 f5 47. exf6 {His king steps to f3, your pawn pushes to f5, but his pawn recaptures on f6 — his king arrives back in time. You are lost.}) (45... f6 {Opening a line on the other wing with f6 abandons the runners on the kingside.} 46. exf6 Kxf6 47. Ke4 {His pawn captures on f6, your king recaptures, and his king strolls in to e4 — far too slow to save your runners. You are lost.}) (45... h3 {Pushing the h pawn before your king is in place lets the rook simply eat it.} 46. Rxg3 {His rook simply captures the pawn on g3. You are lost. [%cal Rg2g3]}) 46. Ra2 {His rook heads off hunting down the a file.} 46... h3 {Side by side on the third row now, and his rook cannot capture either pawn without losing the other. [%csl Gg3,Gh3]} (46... Kh5 {Backing your king off to h5 gives his king exactly the tempo it needs to get back in front of the pawns.} 47. Kf3 f6 48. exf6 {His king steps to f3, your pawn pushes to f6, but his pawn recaptures there and his king arrives in time. You are lost.}) (46... g2 {Pushing the other pawn, to g2, moves it before it has any support.} 47. Rxg2+ {His rook simply captures the undefended pawn on g2, with check. You are lost. [%cal Ra2g2]}) (46... Kf5 {Your king wanders off toward f5, heading nowhere useful.} 47. Kf3 f6 48. Re2 {His king steps to f3, your pawn pushes to f6, and his rook settles on e2 — the pawns are lost without your king to guard them.}) (46... f6 {Breaking open the kingside with f6 a move too soon lets his pawn strike back.} 47. exf6 h3 48. f7 {His pawn captures on f6, your h pawn pushes on, but his own pawn on f7 queens first — yours is still two squares from home. You are lost.}) 47. Ra8 {His rook swings all the way round to a8, coming in behind your pawns.} 47... h2 {One pawn on the second row, one on the third, and his king is miles away from either of them. [%csl Gh2,Gg3][%tail]} (47... g2 {Pushing the wrong pawn first, to g2, lets him round up both of them.} 48. Rg8+ Kh5 49. Kf2 {His rook checks from g8, your king steps to h5, and his king strolls over to f2 — he rounds up both pawns. You are lost.}) (47... f6 {Moving the wrong pawn, on f6, hands him the tempo he needs.} 48. exf6 h2 49. Rg8+ Kh5 50. f7 {His pawn captures on f6, your h pawn pushes to h2, his rook checks from g8, your king steps to h5, and his own pawn on f7 beats yours to the last row by a single tempo. You are lost.}) (47... Kh5 {Stepping your king to h5 a move too soon lets a check on h8 herd it out of the way.} 48. Rh8+ Kg6 49. Rxh3 {His rook checks from h8, your king steps to g6, and his rook scoops up the pawn on h3. You are lost.}) (47... Kf5 {Trying the same wandering idea with the king again wastes the move.} 48. Rg8 f6 49. Rxg3 {His rook drops to g8, your pawn pushes to f6, and his rook scoops up the g3 pawn — the second runner is gone too.}) 48. Rh8 48... g2 49. Rg8+ 49... Kh5 50. Rxg2 50... h1=Q {And the other pawn queens anyway, on h1. His rook could never watch two doors at once. That is winning. [%csl Gh1]} 0-1


[Event "Imbalanced endgames: two squares, not one"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Role "exercise"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/d2Tnwe56/Rt7dsggB"]
[FEN "8/8/5k1P/p4P1K/8/1P6/6P1/2n5 b - - 2 41"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/5k1P/p4P1K/8/1P6/6P1/2n5_b_-_-_2_41"]

{A knight and a pawn face three pawns today. Count the moves this race takes, not the pieces on the board.}
41... Nxb3 {His knight captures the pawn on b3, and his own a pawn starts running. One move wins here for you, and only one. [%csl Ra5]} 42. g4 {Two squares in one go! Your pawn jumps to g4, so it reaches g5 with check next move. [%csl Gg4]} (42. g3 {One square instead of two costs you a whole tempo — the check arrives a move too late.} 42... Nd2 {His knight settles on d2, and now your pawn needs an extra move it simply does not have. You are lost.}) (42. h7 {Pushing the runner pawn to h7 first, before the check, lets his king simply step in front of it.} 42... Kg7 {His king steps in front on g7, and the pawn is stopped. You are lost. [%cal Rf6g7]}) (42. Kg4 {Bringing your king over to g4 instead of pushing the pawn loses the tempo race.} 42... a4 {His pawn is simply quicker. You are lost.}) (42. Kh4 {The same idea, a square lower on h4, is just as slow.} 42... a4 {His a pawn is already gone before your king gets anywhere near it.}) 42... a4 {His pawn runs too, racing yours toward its own queening square.} 43. g5+ {Check! He has to answer it, and that buys your h pawn the free move it needs. [%cal Gg5f6]} (43. h7 {Pushing the runner to h7 one move too early lets his king catch it.} 43... Kg7 {His king catches the pawn on g7. You are lost. [%cal Rf6g7]}) (43. Kh4 {A quiet king move to h4 wastes time you do not have in this race.} 43... Nd4 {His knight jumps to d4, and without a check you have already lost the tempo race.}) 43... Kxf5 {He takes your pawn on f5 and steps aside — the race is not over yet.} 44. h7 {The pawn is one square from home, and his knight is much too far away to catch it. [%csl Gh7][%tail]} (44. g6 {Pushing the wrong pawn, on g6, gives his king the chance to stop both of them.} 44... Kf6 {His king steps to f6 and stops the pair of them together. You are lost.}) (44. Kh4 {Waiting with the king on h4 costs you a tempo you do not have to spare.} 44... Kg6 {His king gets in front of the pawn on g6. You are lost.}) 44... Nd4 45. h8=Q {Queen on h8! A knight and a lone pawn are no match for a king, a queen and a pawn.} 1-0


[Event "Imbalanced endgames: the knight blocks the check"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Role "exercise"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/d2Tnwe56/AbZjiZMG"]
[FEN "8/6p1/P6p/8/1KN1kp2/7r/8/8 b - - 2 46"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/6p1/P6p/8/1KN1kp2/7r/8/8_b_-_-_2_46"]

{A knight and a passed pawn take on a rook today, and the checks are about to start coming from every side.}
46... Rh1 {His rook drops to the back row to watch your pawn. One move wins here for you, and only one.} 47. a7 {Push to a7! Now the checks start, and you need to know exactly where your king is going. [%cal Ga7a8]} (47. Nd2+ {Checking first with the knight from d2 spends the piece's one useful move on nothing.} 47... Kd3 48. Nb3 Re1 {His king steps to d3, your knight gets chased to b3, and his rook settles on e1 — the pawn is stuck for good. You are lost.}) (47. Nb2 {Guarding the queening square from a distance on b2 is too slow to matter.} 47... Rc1 48. a7 Rc8 {His rook swings to c1, your pawn still pushes to a7, but his rook gets in front on c8 first. You are lost.}) (47. Kb5 {Escorting the pawn with the king to b5 instead of pushing it wastes the tempo.} 47... Rb1+ 48. Kc6 Ra1 {His rook checks from b1, your king steps to c6, and his rook swings round to a1 — the rook simply wins the pawn. You are lost.}) (47. Na5 {Covering the pawn with the knight from a5 leaves the queening square unguarded.} 47... Rd1 48. a7 Rd8 {His rook swings to d1, your pawn still pushes to a7, but his rook gets round behind it to d8. You are lost.}) (47. Ka5 {Standing your own king in front of the pawn on a5 blocks your own plan.} 47... Ra1+ 48. Kb6 Rxa6+ {His rook checks from a1, your king steps to b6, and his rook simply captures the pawn on a6, with check. You are lost. [%cal Ra1a6]}) 47... Rb1+ {The first check arrives, from b1. Which square keeps your pawn alive?} 48. Ka4 {The only square that works. Now he will check you again, straight down the a file. [%csl Ga4]} (48. Nb2 {Blocking the check right now with the knight on b2 costs you the piece for nothing useful.} 48... Rxb2+ 49. Ka3 Re2 {His rook captures the knight on b2, with check, your king steps to a3, and his rook settles safely on e2. Nothing defends the pawn there. Level at best.}) (48. Kc3 {Walking the king away from the checks to c3 abandons the pawn.} 48... Ra1 49. Kd2 Rxa7 {His rook swings to a1, your king steps to d2, and his rook simply captures the pawn on a7. You are lost.}) (48. Kc5 {Going the other direction, to c5, gains nothing over c3.} 48... Ra1 49. Nd6+ Kd3 {His rook swings to a1, your knight checks from d6, but his king steps to d3 — the check gains you nothing, and his rook on a1 still eats the pawn.}) (48. Ka3 {One square wrong: from a3 the check still herds your king to b4, and the pawn falls just the same.} 48... Ra1+ 49. Kb4 Rxa7 {His rook checks from a1, your king is pushed to b4, and his rook captures the pawn on a7 anyway. One square out, and the pawn is gone. You are lost.}) (48. Ka5 {Also one square out, on a5, leads to the very same ending.} 48... Ra1+ 49. Kb6 Rxa7 {His rook checks from a1, your king steps to b6, and his rook captures the pawn on a7 all the same. You are lost.}) 48... Ra1+ {Check down the a file, with your own pawn standing right behind you.} 49. Na3 {The knight throws itself into the check on a3. If his rook captures it, nothing at all is left between your pawn and a8. [%csl Ga3][%tail]} (49. Kb4 {Stepping the king out of the check to b4 instead of blocking leaves the pawn undefended.} 49... Rxa7 {His rook simply captures the pawn on a7, and it is gone. You are lost. [%cal Ra1a7]}) (49. Kb5 {The same idea, one square further, still leaves the pawn hanging.} 49... Rxa7 {His rook captures the pawn on a7 just the same. Gone, and you are lost.}) (49. Kb3 {Your king slides to b3, but nothing at all is watching the pawn on a7 any more. [%csl Ra7]} 49... Rxa7 {His rook simply collects the pawn on a7. Your knight had a job to do, and it never got done. You are lost.}) 49... g5 50. a8=Q+ {A new queen appears on a8, with check, and the knight on a3 still stands in his rook's way. [%csl Ga8]} 1-0


[Event "Imbalanced endgames: one bishop, two wings"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Role "exercise"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/d2Tnwe56/1Qj3hgWt"]
[FEN "3B4/5kp1/6p1/3K1p2/8/2p4P/8/8 w - - 1 48"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/3B4/5kp1/6p1/3K1p2/8/2p4P/8/8_w_-_-_1_48"]

{Two passed pawns, one on each wing, take on a lonely bishop that cannot possibly watch both of them.}
48. Ke5 {His king steps up to hold back the f pawn. You have two runners, and only one order gets them home.} 48... f4 {This one first — the f pawn steps out as the decoy, and the bishop needs to deal with it if it wants any hope of stopping your pawns. [%cal Gf4f1]} (48... c2 {Pushing the far pawn first, on c3 to c2, looks obvious, but the bishop is ready for it.} 49. Bg5 f4 50. Bxf4 {His bishop comes across to g5, your other pawn pushes to f4, and his bishop simply captures it there — from f4 it watches c1 as well. Level. [%cal Rf4c1]}) (48... g5 {Pushing on the far wing with g5 hands his king the chance to grab both runners.} 49. Kxf5 g4 50. hxg4 {His king captures the pawn on f5, your other pawn pushes to g4, and his own h pawn simply takes it. You have handed over both of them. You are lost.}) (48... Kg8 {A waiting move on g8 gives him all the time he needs to get organised.} 49. Bg5 c2 50. h4 {His bishop comes to g5, your pawn pushes to c2, and his own pawn gets moving on h4. He gets time to organise, and you are much worse.}) (48... Ke8 {Walking your king over to e8 instead of pushing a pawn wastes the move.} 49. Bg5 Kd7 50. Kd5 {His bishop comes to g5, your king steps to d7, and his king reaches d5 — he holds everything together. Level.}) 49. Bg5 {The bishop comes across to g5 to deal with the decoy pawn.} 49... f3 {Yes — now the bishop's job is to stay tied to the f file, or your pawn queens unopposed.} (49... c2 {Pushing the far pawn now instead, the bishop is ready and waiting for it.} 50. Bxf4 g5 51. Be3 {His bishop captures the pawn on f4, your other pawn pushes to g5, and his bishop settles on e3 — from there it watches c1 too, and your far pawn can never get past it. Level. [%cal Re3c1]}) (49... Ke8 {The king goes for a walk to e8 instead of pushing the pawn.} 50. Kxf4 Kd7 51. Ke3 {His king captures the pawn on f4, your king steps to d7, and his king reaches e3. Your f pawn is gone. You are lost.}) (49... Kg8 {Your king marks time on g8, and his own simply strolls in to eat the f pawn.} 50. Kxf4 c2 51. Ke4 {His king captures the pawn on f4, your other pawn pushes to c2, and his king reaches e4 in time. The pawn falls, and you are lost.}) (49... Kf8 {f8 is no safer than g8 — his king still gets there, and the f pawn goes with it.} 50. Kxf4 Ke8 51. Ke3 {His king captures the pawn on f4, your king steps to e8, and his king reaches e3. Your last pawn is gone, and his king strolls back to stop the other one too.}) 50. Be3 {The bishop drops back to e3, the one square that can watch both queening squares, c1 and f2, at once.} 50... c2 {Your pawn steps to c2, and the bishop on e3 is handed two jobs at once, c1 and f2. It cannot hold both. [%csl Gc2][%tail]} (50... f2 {Giving up the decoy pawn on f2 too soon frees the bishop for the other job.} 51. Bxf2 g5 52. Kf5 {His bishop captures the pawn on f2, your other pawn pushes to g5, and his king reaches f5. The bishop is free again. You are lost.}) (50... g5 {A move on the wrong wing, pushing g5, wastes the tempo you needed.} 51. Ke4 g4 52. hxg4 {His king steps to e4, your pawn pushes to g4, and his own h pawn simply takes it. Your last runner is gone.}) (50... Ke7 {Moving the king instead of the pawn gives him the tempo he needs.} 51. Kf4 Ke6 52. Kxf3 {His king steps to f4, your king steps to e6, and his king simply captures the pawn on f3. You are lost.}) (50... Kg8 {Waiting again on g8 gives away the other pawn too — his king marches to f4 and then takes on f3.} 51. Kf4 Kf7 52. Kxf3 {His king steps to f4, your king shuffles to f7, and his king scoops up the pawn on f3 all the same. Nothing is left to push.}) 51. Ke4 51... f2 52. Bxf2 52... c1=Q {A new queen on c1, bought with the f pawn his bishop had to take on f2. Two wings were simply one too many for him to watch.} 0-1
